WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 260: Walt Whitman: Leaves of Grass, 'Song of Myself', and the Bard of Democracy

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.960
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the roaring, oceanic voice of American democracy.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:22.080
[Rebecca] The Brooklyn printer and poet who tore up the rules of poetry to celebrate the common laborer and the wounded soldier.

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Walt Whitman.

6
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:44.320
[George] Born in May 1819 on a windswept Long Island farm in West Hills, New York, a landscape he called by its ancient native name, Pominock, Walter Whitman was the second of nine children in a large, working-class family steeped in radical Quaker ideals.

7
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:57.600
[George] Moving to the booming maritime city of Brooklyn at age four, young Walt ended his formal education at age 11 to work as an errand boy for a law office, before learning the art of typesetting at the Long Island Patriot.

8
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:09.280
[George] As a teenage printer and itinerant country schoolteacher in rural Queens, Whitman developed a deep, lifelong love for the English language, opera, Shakespeare, and the Homeric epics.

9
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:41.840
[George] By his twenties, Whitman was a dynamic crusading journalist and editor of the Brooklyn Daily Eagle, walking the bustling streets of Manhattan and riding the Fulton Ferry across the East River, mesmerized by the vibrant throngs of deckhands, omnibus drivers, seamstresses, blacksmiths, and dockworkers, absorbing the energy, slang, and raw physical beauty of the working-class metropolis, Whitman began filling small leather notebooks with long, rolling lines of unrhymed, cadence-driven free verse.

10
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:48.080
[George] Seeking an entirely new poetic form worthy of the vast continental expanse of American democracy.

11
00:01:48.080 --> 00:02:02.040
[George] Young Walt's childhood on the fertile plains of Pomanoch and the bustling streets of Brooklyn immersed him in the dual rhythms of pastoral nature and urban industrial energy, instilling in him an all-encompassing love for the physical diversity of America.

12
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:14.440
[George] His early experience as a printer and crusading journalist taught him to view language as an organic, living medium that belonged to the blacksmith and the omnibus driver rather than the academic ivory tower.

13
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:27.880
[George] On July 4, 1855, 36-year-old Walt Whitman published the first edition of Leaves of Grass at his own expense, setting much of the lead type with his own hands in the print shop of the Rome Brothers in Brooklyn.

14
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.200
[George] The slim green volume was revolutionary in every dimension.

15
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:45.640
[George] The title page contained no author name, replaced instead by a striking steel engraving of a bearded Whitman standing in working man's trousers, an unbuttoned shirt exposing his undershirt, and a jaunty tilted hat.

16
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:58.120
[George] Breaking completely with European metre, rhyme and polite decorum, the opening twelve untitled poems, later titled Song of Myself, sounded an electrifying, audacious barbaric yawp.

17
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.240
[George] I celebrate myself and sing myself, and what I assume you shall assume.

18
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.680
[George] For every atom belonging to me is good belongs to you.

19
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:19.800
[George] Whitman boldly celebrated the sacred holiness of the human body, physical sexuality, and the divine equality of all living creatures, declaring that a blade of grass was as miraculous as the journey of the stars.

20
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:31.000
[George] While conservative critics condemned the book as obscene, reckless trash, Ralph Waldo Emerson read it in Concord, Massachusetts, writing back an ecstatic, legendary letter.

21
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.600
[George] I greet you at the beginning of a great career, which yet must have had a long foreground somewhere.

22
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:51.200
[George] For such a start, Leaves of Grass was an intellectual revolution that exploded the polite, rhymed conventions of 19th century poetry, introducing a cascading, free-verse line that surged forward like the waves of the Atlantic Ocean.

23
00:03:51.440 --> 00:04:06.320
[George] Emerson's enthusiastic letter of praise provided the young poet with vital moral validation, encouraging Whitman to expand his vision of an all-inclusive democratic commonwealth where every laborer, farmer, and mother was recognized as sacred.

24
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:23.840
[George] In December 1862, following the catastrophic Union defeat at the Battle of Fredericksburg, Virginia, Whitman read the name of his younger brother, First Lieutenant George Washington Whitman of the 51st New York Volunteers, in the casualty lists published in the New York Tribune.

25
00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:35.200
[George] Petrified with dread, 43-year-old Walt immediately boarded a train south, searching military hospitals in Washington before making his way to the snowy frontlines at Falmouth.

26
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.680
[George] Whitman found his brother alive, having suffered only a superficial cheek wound from a shell fragment.

27
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:48.320
[George] But outside the regimental hospital tent, Whitman beheld a scene of horrific carnage that shattered his soul.

28
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:58.880
[George] A heap of severed human arms and legs stacked against the canvas wall like cords of firewood, while hundreds of shattered young men lay groaning on frozen pine boughs in the snow.

29
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:07.680
[George] Escorting a boatload of amputees and wounded soldiers back up the Potomac to Washington, Whitman experienced a profound spiritual awakening.

30
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:13.680
[George] He resolved to remain in the national capital to dedicate his life to nursing the wounded of both armies.

31
00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] The gruesome scene outside the field hospital at Fredericksburg transformed Whitman's artistic life, shifting his poetic focus from abstract democratic philosophy to direct, visceral service to suffering human beings.

32
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:40.040
[George] Escorting boatloads of shivering, mutilated soldiers up the cold waters of the Potomac River, Whitman realized that the true heroism of the American nation was written in the blood and courage of its common soldiers.

33
00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:56.840
[George] For the remainder of the Civil War, Whitman lived in a modest rented attic room on M Street in Washington, supporting himself with a minor clerkship in the Army Paymaster's office, and spending every afternoon and evening in the sprawling military hospital wards that filled the capital.

34
00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:12.520
[George] Carrying a large haversack packed with fresh fruit, preserves, tobacco, writing paper, postage stamps, and whorehound candy purchased with small donations from friends, the tall, grey-bearded poet moved quietly down the long aisles of cots.

35
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.400
[George] He was not a doctor, but an angel of emotional and spiritual mercy.

36
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:35.480
[George] He cleaned and dressed putrid, gangrenous wounds, held the hands of 19-year-old boys during agonizing surgical amputations without anesthesia, read biblical passages and poetry aloud, played checkers with convalescents, and sat beside the dying through the dark midnight hours.

37
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:45.880
[George] He wrote hundreds of poignant, heartbreaking letters home to mothers across Ohio, Maine, Virginia, and Georgia, reporting the final loving words of their dying sons.

38
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:57.960
[George] Historians estimate that Whitman personally comforted over 80,000 wounded Union and Confederate soldiers, pouring his experiences into his searing Civil War poetry collection, drum taps.

39
00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:11.240
[George] Whitman's selfless ministry in the Washington hospital wards earned him the enduring love of thousands of soldiers from Maine to Georgia, who found in the good gray poet an affectionate brother, father, and spiritual comforter.

40
00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:21.360
[George] The hundreds of letters he wrote to anxious mothers in remote prairie cabins documented the quiet dignity and sacrifice of young men who died with whispers of love on their lips.

41
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:34.400
[George] During his years in Washington, Whitman regularly watched President Abraham Lincoln riding on horseback down Pennsylvania Avenue, flanked by cavalry guards, exchanging respectful bows with the president.

42
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:45.120
[George] Whitman revered Lincoln as the supreme, tragic embodiment of American democracy, a rugged Western commoner carrying the weight of a divided continent on his sorrowful shoulders.

43
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:57.920
[George] On the morning of April 15, 1865, when news flashed that Lincoln had been assassinated at Ford's Theater, Whitman was at his mother's home in Brooklyn, where the purple lilacs were blooming in the yard.

44
00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:33.600
[George] Devastated by the martyrdom of his beloved president, Whitman composed two immortal elegies, O Captain, My Captain, a structured rhyming ballad celebrating the triumph of the preserved union alongside the tragic fall of its helmsman, which became the most famous schoolroom poem in America, and the sublime, symphonic pastoral masterpiece, when Liloc's last in the dooryard bloomed, weaving together the sensory motifs of the purple lilac, the drooping western evening star Venus, and the solitary thrush singing its sweet death hymn in the dark cedar swamps.

45
00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:46.960
[George] Whitman's profound spiritual reverence for Abraham Lincoln captured the tragic sorrow of the Civil War, recognizing in the tall, sorrowful president the ultimate martyr who died to preserve the continental union of free states.

46
00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:58.160
[George] In When Lilac's Last in the Dooryard Bloomed, Whitman elevated Lincoln's death into a universal hymn to spring, mortality, and the eternal renewal of the democratic soul.

47
00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:07.320
[George] In January 1873, the physical toll of hospital infections, emotional strain, and tireless overwork caught up with Whitman.

48
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.400
[George] He suffered a severe paralytic stroke that crippled his left side.

49
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:22.360
[George] When his beloved mother died months later, the devastated poet moved to Camden, New Jersey, to live with his brother George, eventually purchasing his first and only home.

50
00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:28.840
[George] A humble, two-story clapboard cottage at 328 Mickle Street near the Delaware River Ferry.

51
00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:40.680
[George] Confined to an oak-rocking chair in his second-floor study piled high with mountains of books, letters and manuscript scraps, the good gray poet became an international literary shrine.

52
00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:49.880
[George] European writers like Oscar Wilde, Bram Stoker and Edward Carpenter made pilgrimages across the Atlantic to sit at his feet and drink tea.

53
00:09:49.880 --> 00:10:10.280
[George] Despite his physical infirmity, Whitman maintained an unshakable, sunny optimism about the future of human democracy, continuing to revise, expand, and polish leaves of grass through nine distinct lifetime editions, culminating in the monumental Deathbed Edition of 1891-1892.

54
00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:25.240
[George] Confined to his Mickle Street Cottage in Camden, New Jersey, Whitman remained a radiant, welcoming beacon to literary pilgrims from around the world, who traveled across the Atlantic to sit in his rocking chair and discuss poetry and philosophy.

55
00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:36.040
[George] His tireless revisions of Leaves of Grass produced a monumental poetic cathedral that documented the turbulent growth of a young republic through civil war and industrial revolution.

56
00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:44.840
[George] Walt Whitman died peacefully on the rainy evening of March 26, 1892, in his Mickle Street cottage at age 72.

57
00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:56.480
[George] Four days later, over 4,000 admirers and ordinary citizens crowded Harley Cemetery in Camden to lay the poet to rest in a massive granite mausoleum he had designed into the hillside.

58
00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:00.960
[George] Whitman achieved what no other writer in American history had accomplished.

59
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:13.120
[George] He created a cosmic, democratic poetry capable of embracing every aspect of the human experience, the triumphant and the tragic, the sublime and the ordinary, the physical and the spiritual.

60
00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:21.920
[George] He dissolved the barriers between races, classes, and nations, inviting all of humanity to join his open road of universal brotherhood.

61
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:31.680
[George] As Whitman immortalized in the closing lines of Song of Myself, his spirit is not buried in cold granite, but lives in the vibrant dirt beneath our boots.

62
00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.200
[George] I bequeath myself to the dirt to grow from the grass I love.

63
00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:38.240
[George] If you want me again, look for me under your boots holes.

64
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.920
[George] Missing me one place search another.

65
00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:46.080
[George] I stop somewhere waiting for you.

66
00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:58.720
[George] At his funeral in Camden, thousands of working men, dockworkers, poets, and neighbors gathered in solemn tribute, celebrating the passing of a visionary who had given voice to the unuttered longings of humanity.

67
00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:08.640
[George] Walt Whitman's barbaric yaw continues to echo over the roofs of the world, reminding us that we are all interconnected in the great, democratic journey of life.

68
00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:12.400
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

69
00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:19.440
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 261.

70
00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:25.600
[Rebecca] Henry David Thoreau, Walden Pond, Civil Disobedience, and the Cabins in the Conquered Woods.

71
00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:28.000
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

